                        "review": "Tavern Keeper is an excellent game, built with a lot of love, polish, and community input.\r\n\r\nI last played before Update 1, and have just come back for Update 2 - the level of improvement is astonishing.  The Gugamesh dirt cleaning death spiral is gone, or at least, avoidable!\r\n\r\nThe NPC behavior and path finding is top notch at this point, lacking most of the frustrations seen with other games.  There are endless opportunities to optimize your tavern but you are never lacking the knowledge or the means to address the basics.  The UI is rich and well thought out.  The voice acting and writing are hysterical (kind of dry monty python / discworld type british humor - tons of fantasy references and in-jokes).  \r\n\r\nStorybook events interrupt the \"watch numbers go up\" stages of gameplay and are a delightful add, with MANY unique scripted events to encounter.\r\n\r\nThe design mode is pretty amazing.  You can customize and improve any in game furniture or create new decorations out of any combination of fully scalable/transformable objects the game comes with (like >500 feels like?) which provides higher decor scores to boost your tavern's appeal ingame.  You could do nothing else but decorate your tavern like you were playing The Sims - or choose to not engage with the system at all and use the plentiful ingame decor or decor made by others.  \r\n\r\nOverall, this is a cozy sim with all the sharp edges well ground down and documented/tutorialed at this point.  Simple-(ish?) to play, but there is seemingly endless depth and replayability when it comes to optimizing your staff, prices, logistics, building layout, and furniture choices, and difficulty.  Complexity naturally scales with Tavern growth, which gives the player full control of the challenges and goals they want to take on and at what pace.  You can totally cheese this like an old school RPG and spend 30 ingame days (leveling) building gold before story quests, if you want to.  \r\n\r\n...which probably leads to my comment that they could improve by providing more gold sinks at higher stars/later levels as this choice of play method has deflated my satisfaction in the 4th region so far.\r\n\r\nFor fans of The Sims, Rimworld (weird pairing but not kidding), Theme Hospital, Dungeon Keeper.  This takes the lessons of all these and more into a wonderful ratatouille of homage to the past and if I may be so bold...\r\n\r\nThis is the \"Baldur's Gate 3\" of Sim games.  Not in terms of complexity (Go away, Paradox, you're drunk) but in setting a new gold standard and model for the industry like how Rimworld perfected scheduling/work priority.",
